# A first cipher: every letter of a word moves the same number of places along the alphabet, done by
# hand and by a program that walks a list, with an if for letters that go round past Z.
lesson coding-a-secret-code-that-shifts v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=3 subject=coding levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A secret code that shifts"
  goal "Code and decode a word by moving every letter the same number of places, by hand and with a program that goes round past Z."

  look {
    scene 36x8 {
      row top at=canvas(1, 0) space=3 align=end {
        narrowboat nb windows=3 pots=2
        listbox w name=word code=["set word to list 13, 9, 12, 12"]
      }
    }
    say """
      The lock keeper leaves notes for the boats that only they can read. Every letter moves three
      places on: A becomes D, M becomes P. Kept as numbers, A as 1 up to Z as 26, MILL is the list
      13, 9, 12, 12, and the code adds 3 to each. Letters near the end go round: Y moves on through Z
      to A and B. To read a note, take the same number away. This kind of code is over two thousand
      years old.
      """
  }
  do {
    show coding.shift-by-hand k=0 s=2 easy-s=1 hard-s=3
    level easy {
      worked coding.shift-by-hand k=3 s=1 level=medium
    }
    practice coding.shift-wrap count=1 seed=2
    practice coding.shift-trace count=1 seed=3
    practice coding.shift-missing count=1 seed=4
    practice coding.shift-undo count=1 seed=5
    show coding.shift-build k=0 easy-k=1 hard-k=2
    practice coding.shift-trace count=1 seed=8
    level medium hard {
      practice coding.shift-wrap count=1 seed=9
    }
    level hard {
      practice coding.shift-undo count=1 seed=11
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    practice coding.shift-find-key count=1 seed=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show coding.shift-nights t=3 easy-t=0 hard-t=5
  }
  remember {
    say "To code, move every letter the same number on. To read it, move them back. After Z comes A again."
  }

  grown-ups """
    This is a shift cipher, the code Suetonius says Julius Caesar used with a shift of three
    (recalled from Suetonius, Life of Julius Caesar, 56). Text is kept as numbers, as in grade four's
    lanterns, so a code is a rule applied to every number in a list, and the program here walks the
    list by place with a counter, adds the shift, and uses an if to take 26 away from a number past
    Z. The slips to watch for are forgetting to go round after Z, and decoding by adding instead of
    taking away. In the two-star question the child works backwards from a word and its code to the
    shift; a letter that went round gives a difference 26 too small. In the three-star question the
    notice board comes back to its first message after 26 divided by what the shift and 26 have in
    common nights, so a shift of 4 takes 13 nights, not 26 and not 6 and a half. If the child is
    stuck, write the alphabet round a paper plate and turn an inner ring. Off the page, send a coded
    note to someone at home and give them the shift. A shift code is easy to break, since there are
    only 25 shifts to try, which is why real codes today use much longer keys. This meets CSTA
    2-NI-06, "Apply multiple methods of encryption to model the secure transmission of information,"
    and builds on the lists of grade five's first coding lessons.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the shifts are 1 to 3, one word is coded in the teacher's pen, the program's letters never pass Z, the missing line and the note to undo use small shifts, the build moves the robot 7 squares, the shift to find has no letter past Z, and the notice board comes back after 2 or 13 nights."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the shifts reach 5 to 9, the letters traced always pass Z, the note to undo uses a shift of 4 or 5, the build moves the robot 11 squares, the shift to find starts with a letter that went past Z, and the notice board moves 6 or 8 places a night."
  }
}
